"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][4]={"university_physics_en:M4-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M4-L1 – Simple Harmonic Oscillations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Forces, acceleration, differential\nequations and energy from M2/M3. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the equation of motion\nfrom a linear restoring force and determine period, phase and\nenergy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>The Linear Oscillator<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>oscillation<\/strong> is motion about a stable equilibrium.\nFor small displacements, the restoring force is often approximately\nproportional to the displacement. In an ideal mass–spring system\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_x=-kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe minus sign indicates that the force points back toward equilibrium.\nWith neither friction nor external driving, <strong>simple harmonic\nmotion<\/strong> results. Position, velocity and acceleration are\nperiodic; velocity vanishes at turning points even though the magnitude\nof acceleration may be greatest there. Not every periodic motion is\nharmonic: the linear equation of motion is essential.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Equation of Motion and Energy<\/h2>\n<p>Newton’s second law gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot x=-kx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe trial solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega_0t+\\varphi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas second derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot x=-\\omega_0^2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMatching coefficients yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ddot x+\\omega_0^2x=0,\\qquad \\omega_0=\\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}},\\qquad T=\\frac{2\\pi}{\\omega_0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The general real solution can also be expressed as a combination of\nsine and cosine.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes amplitude,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninitial phase and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nangular frequency in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrather than hertz. The energy remains constant:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\tfrac12m\\dot x^2+\\tfrac12kx^2=\\tfrac12kA^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This describes the continuous exchange of kinetic and elastic\npotential energy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Mass–Spring System<\/h2>\n<p>A mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0.50\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non an ideal spring with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=200\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis released at a displacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.10\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom equilibrium. Then\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.50<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=\\sqrt{200/0.50}=20\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.314<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi/20\\approx0.314\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.18<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f\\approx3.18\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(0)=A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x(0)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe trajectory is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(t)=0.10\\cos(20t)\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts peak speed is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\omega_0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe total energy is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.0\\,\\mathrm J<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram distinguishes zero crossings, turning points and one full\nperiod.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_125_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Time-dependent displacement showing amplitude and one complete period\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Time-dependent displacement showing\namplitude and one complete period<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Amplitude is the distance from equilibrium; one period repeats\nthe pattern.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=50\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The amplitude is doubled with the same spring. How do peak velocity\nand energy change?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the magnitude of acceleration greatest at a turning\npoint?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>50<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=\\sqrt{50/2}=5.0\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.257<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=2\\pi/5\\approx1.257\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\max}=A\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles, while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=\\tfrac12kA^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nquadruples.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a=-\\omega_0^2x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|a|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis largest when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|x|=A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model Limits and Phase Space<\/h2>\n<p>The ideal solution assumes constant mass, a linear spring, no losses\nand fixed boundary conditions. At large amplitudes, a restoring force\nmay become nonlinear; the small-angle approximation for a physical\npendulum is valid only within an appropriate range. In state space,\nplotting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na lossless harmonic oscillator traces a closed ellipse. With friction, a\ntrajectory is no longer closed. For this linear system the\n<strong>natural frequency<\/strong> depends on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot on the selected amplitude.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Simple harmonic motion is the reference model for oscillations. We\nnext add losses and periodic forcing.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Damped and Driven\nOscillations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 15 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 15.1 Simple Harmonic Motion; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L1 – Simple Harmonic Oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-03sc-physics-iii-vibrations-and-waves-fall-2016/pages/resource-index/\">8.03SC Physics III – Resource Index (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Part I, Lectures 1–6 and 9; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L1 – Simple Harmonic Oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M4-L2 – Damped and Driven Oscillations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Simple harmonic oscillator and\ncomplex exponential notation. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare damping regimes and the\nfrequency response of a linearly driven oscillator quantitatively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dissipation and External Driving<\/h2>\n<p>Real oscillators lose mechanical energy through friction, fluid drag\nor internal material losses. A common approximation is a damping force\nproportional to velocity,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_d=-b\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf an additional sinusoidal external force acts, stored energy,\ndissipation and periodically supplied work compete.\n<strong>Resonance<\/strong> is a pronounced dependence of the linear\nresponse on the driving frequency; it does not necessarily mean an\nunbounded amplitude. Damping controls both the free motion and the\nheight and shape of the frequency-response curve.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transient and Steady-State Solutions<\/h2>\n<p>For a linear oscillator of constant mass,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>+<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot x+b\\dot x+kx=F_0\\cos(\\Omega t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=b/(2m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=\\sqrt{k/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe characteristic polynomial of the free motion is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r^2+2\\gamma r+\\omega_0^2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the underdamped case\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma&lt;\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe free solution oscillates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_d=\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2-\\gamma^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis critical damping. A steady-state ansatz gives displacement\namplitude<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(\\Omega)=\\frac{F_0/m}{\\sqrt{(\\omega_0^2-\\Omega^2)^2+(2\\gamma\\Omega)^2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The transient part decays for positive damping, while the drive\nsustains the steady-state response.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Resonance Curve<\/h2>\n<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=100\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_0=1.0\\,\\mathrm N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=10\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1.0\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe steady response amplitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(0)=F_0/k=0.010\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega=\\omega_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.050<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(\\omega_0)=1/(2\\cdot1\\cdot10)=0.050\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe displacement maximum occurs at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>9.90<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Omega_r=\\sqrt{\\omega_0^2-2\\gamma^2}\\approx9.90\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0^2&gt;2\\gamma^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram therefore places the peak slightly below the undamped\nnatural frequency.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_246_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two normalised resonance curves for weak and stronger damping\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two normalised resonance curves for weak\nand stronger damping<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Weaker damping gives a taller, narrower peak for the same model\nparameters.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Compute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=4.0\\,\\mathrm{kg/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_0=8.0\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1.0\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncompute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(\\Omega)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfinite when damping is present?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=b/(2m)=1.0\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>64<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>63<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>7.94<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_d=\\sqrt{64-1}=\\sqrt{63}\\approx7.94\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The positive term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(2\\gamma\\Omega)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprevents the denominator from vanishing near resonance; dissipative\npower limits the steady amplitude.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>What Resonance Does Not Mean<\/h2>\n<p>For weak damping, the quality factor is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\approx\\omega_0/(2\\gamma)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit characterises a narrow, pronounced resonance. A different measured\nquantity, such as velocity rather than displacement, can peak at another\nfrequency. Nonlinearity, variable friction and material limits can cause\ndepartures from the model. Resonance is not always damaging or always\nuseful: it enables filters and instruments but must also be considered\nwhen designing structures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The response to driving is frequency selective. Coupled oscillators\nhave multiple characteristic modes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Coupled Oscillators and Normal\nModes<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/15-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 15 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 15.1 Simple Harmonic Motion; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L2 – Damped and Driven Oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-03sc-physics-iii-vibrations-and-waves-fall-2016/pages/resource-index/\">8.03SC Physics III – Resource Index (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Part I, Lectures 1–6 and 9; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L2 – Damped and Driven Oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M4-L3 – Coupled Oscillators and Normal Modes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Harmonic oscillator, linear algebra\nand eigenvalues. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive normal modes of two\ncoupled masses and interpret initial motion as superposition.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From One to Several Degrees of Freedom<\/h2>\n<p>When two systems interact through a spring or another coupling, their\nmotions are no longer independent. One oscillator can transfer energy to\nthe other. For small displacements, many such systems admit linear\nequations of motion. A <strong>normal mode<\/strong> is a simple pattern\nin which all coordinates oscillate at one common natural frequency with\nfixed amplitude ratios. General motion is a superposition of several\nnormal modes. The same idea extends from two masses to lattice\nvibrations in solids.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Eigenvalue Problem for Two Masses<\/h2>\n<p>Consider identical masses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach attached to a fixed wall by a spring\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand connected to the other mass by a coupling spring\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir displacements obey<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>m<\/mi><msub><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̈<\/mo><\/mover><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>κ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m\\ddot x_1=-(k+\\kappa)x_1+\\kappa x_2,\\qquad\nm\\ddot x_2=\\kappa x_1-(k+\\kappa)x_2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_i(t)=a_i\\cos(\\omega t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthese become an eigenvalue problem. The vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrepresents in-phase motion, which does not stretch the coupling spring.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe coupling spring changes length, giving<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>k<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>κ<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>m<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+^2=\\frac{k}{m},\\qquad \\omega_-^2=\\frac{k+2\\kappa}{m}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The subscripts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>+<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>−<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote in-phase and out-of-phase modes here, not positive and negative\nfrequencies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Symmetric and Antisymmetric Modes<\/h2>\n<p>Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=1.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=9.0\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=3.5\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+=3.0\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_-=4.0\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf only mass 1 is displaced by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.040\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand both masses start from rest, the motion is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1(t)=0.020[\\cos(3t)+\\cos(4t)]\\,\\mathrm m,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.020<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2(t)=0.020[\\cos(3t)-\\cos(4t)]\\,\\mathrm m.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Initially the sum yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.040<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.040\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor mass 1 and zero for mass 2, as prescribed. The figure illustrates\nthe two eigenpatterns, which determine the motion at all times.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_056_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Identical masses joined by a coupling spring, showing in-phase and out-of-phase modes\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Identical masses joined by a coupling\nspring, showing in-phase and out-of-phase modes<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The coupling spring is unstretched during in-phase\ndisplacement.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What are the eigenfrequencies when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find both frequencies for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2\\,\\mathrm{kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=8\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa=4\\,\\mathrm{N/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does their joint displacement not depend on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>κ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\kappa<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Both modes are degenerate, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+=\\omega_-=\\sqrt{k/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_+=\\sqrt{8/2}=2\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>8<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.83<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_-=\\sqrt{(8+8)/2}=\\sqrt8\\approx2.83\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Joint displacement does not extend the coupling spring, so its\nforces vanish.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Superposition and Energy Transfer<\/h2>\n<p>When eigenfrequencies are close, slow amplitude envelopes and\nperiodic transfer of energy between the masses can occur. This is often\nperceived as <strong>beating<\/strong>. Without damping, total energy\nremains constant even though the energies of individual masses vary. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndegrees of freedom the calculation generalises to mass and stiffness\nmatrices. Eigenvalues give squared angular frequencies and eigenvectors\ndescribe mode shapes. This requires linear coupling about a suitable\nstable equilibrium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Several eigenmodes generate complex motion; in continua they lead to\nspatially extended waves.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Travelling Waves and\nDispersion<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-03sc-physics-iii-vibrations-and-waves-fall-2016/pages/resource-index/\">8.03SC Physics III – Resource Index (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Part I, Lectures 1–6 and 9; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L3 – Coupled Oscillators and Normal Modes · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M4-L4 – Travelling Waves and Dispersion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Derivatives, trigonometric functions\nand coupled oscillators. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish and apply the wave\nequation, phase velocity and group velocity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A Disturbance Travels<\/h2>\n<p>A <strong>wave<\/strong> is a disturbance varying in space and time\nthat can transfer energy and information. In a transverse wave the\ndisplacement is perpendicular to the propagation direction; in a\nlongitudinal wave it is parallel. A basic field is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y(x,t)=A\\cos(kx-\\omega t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith wavenumber\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2\\pi/\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand angular frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=2\\pi f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLocal oscillation at a fixed position is distinct from the spatial\npattern. The material of a medium need not be transported as a whole at\nthe wave speed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Phase and Group<\/h2>\n<p>In an idealised one-dimensional nondispersive medium a scalar\ndisplacement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msup><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial^2y}{\\partial t^2}=v^2\\frac{\\partial^2y}{\\partial x^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For a harmonic wave\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\omega/k=\\lambda f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a <strong>dispersive<\/strong> medium the relation between\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis nonlinear. A fixed phase\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">kx-\\omega t=\\mathrm{const}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntravels at<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=\\frac{\\omega}{k},\\qquad v_{\\mathrm g}=\\frac{\\mathrm d\\omega}{\\mathrm dk}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For narrow-band wave packets, group velocity approximately describes\npropagation of the envelope. It is not necessarily identical to the\ninformation or signal velocity in all circumstances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Dispersive Wave Packets<\/h2>\n<p>Take the mathematical model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega(k)=c k+\\alpha k^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.20\\,\\mathrm{m^2/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>15<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=15+5=20\\,\\mathrm{rad/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=\\omega/k=4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>α<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm g}=c+2\\alpha k=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe values differ because the dispersion is nonlinear. For a narrow-band\npacket in this model, the envelope moves faster than individual phase\npeaks. These numbers illustrate a mathematical dispersion relation, not\na specified physical medium.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_209_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Oscillating carrier wave within a slowly varying envelope\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Oscillating carrier wave within a slowly\nvarying envelope<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In a dispersive medium phase and envelope can move at different\nvelocities.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=50\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.40\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfind the speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof a nondispersive wave.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For the SI-consistent model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=3k^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=2\\,\\mathrm{m^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes group velocity from phase velocity?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\lambda f=20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=3k=6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm g}=6k=12\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe coefficient 3 carries the required SI units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Phase velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{ph}}=\\omega/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntracks a constant-phase point; group velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm g}=\\mathrm d\\omega/\\mathrm dk<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the motion of the envelope of a narrow-band wave packet.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of the Wave Picture<\/h2>\n<p>In some media different frequency components propagate at different\ngroup velocities, so a pulse broadens. The group velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm g}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not automatically equal energy-transport velocity in every\nsituation, for example in strongly absorbing or complex dispersive\nmedia. Mechanical waves additionally depend on density, stress and\nboundary conditions; electromagnetic waves require different field\nequations. The ideal scalar wave equation is therefore a valuable\nreference model, not a universal law for every wave type.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Waves propagate and can superpose. Superposition leads to\ninterference and standing patterns.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Interference and Standing\nWaves<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/16-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 16 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§ 16.1, 16.5; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L4 – Travelling Waves and Dispersion · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-03sc-physics-iii-vibrations-and-waves-fall-2016/pages/part-ii-electromagnetic-waves/lecture-13/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – 8.03SC Lecture 13: Dispersive Medium, Phase Velocity, Group Velocity (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lecture title; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L4 – Travelling Waves and Dispersion · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M4-L5 – Interference and Standing Waves<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Harmonic waves and trigonometric\naddition. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive interference conditions\nand determine the natural frequencies of a string fixed at both\nends.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Superposition as a Linear Principle<\/h2>\n<p>When two waves overlap in a <strong>linear<\/strong> medium, their\ninstantaneous displacements add. For equal frequencies the relative\nphase determines constructive or destructive interference. A stable\nphase relation over the observation interval is required for persistent\ninterference. Two equal-amplitude harmonic waves travelling in opposite\ndirections can generate a <strong>standing wave<\/strong>. Unlike a\ntravelling sinusoid, a standing wave has fixed nodes where displacement\nvanishes at all times.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interference and Boundary Conditions<\/h2>\n<p>Two harmonic waves with the same amplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwavenumber\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand frequency\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nadd as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\cos(kx-\\omega t)+A\\cos(kx+\\omega t)=2A\\cos(kx)\\cos(\\omega t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Their position-dependent amplitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2A|\\cos(kx)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith nodes wherever\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\cos(kx)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an ideal string of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed at both ends, displacement must vanish at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nOnly certain wavelengths and frequencies are then allowed:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda_n=\\frac{2L}{n},\\qquad f_n=\\frac{nv}{2L},\\qquad n=1,2,3,\\ldots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the wave speed on the string, given by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=\\sqrt{F_T/\\mu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an ideal taut string with tension\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand linear mass density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Vibrating String<\/h2>\n<p>A string of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.80\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis under sufficient tension for transverse waves to travel at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>160<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=160\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts fundamental frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=v/(2L)=100\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe second and third harmonics are at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>200<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">300\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe fundamental has nodes at both ends and an antinode at the centre.\nMode two also has a node at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=L/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the spatial shapes of the first three modes at an\ninstant of maximum displacement. The allowed mode shapes do not depend\non the initial excitation amplitude.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_277_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"First three string mode shapes with fixed nodes at both ends\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">First three string mode shapes with fixed\nnodes at both ends<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Mode n has n − 1 interior nodes.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the fundamental frequency of a string fixed at both ends with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>120<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=120\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many interior nodes are present in its fourth normal mode?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two equal-amplitude waves arrive with phase difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>π<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWhat is their sum?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=v/(2L)=60\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">There are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-1=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninterior nodes, plus nodes at both ends.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For equal polarisation and frequency their displacements cancel\nlocally.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Coherence and Real Systems<\/h2>\n<p>A real string has losses, finite bending stiffness and imperfect\nsupports. These change frequencies and shorten the lifetime of its\nmotion. Simple superposition fails for strongly nonlinear waves. For\ninterference one must distinguish the added <strong>field\namplitudes<\/strong> from measured intensity: intensity is generally\nproportional to a time-averaged square and therefore contains cross\nterms. Boundary conditions determine whether a standing wave can be\nsustained.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Interference and boundary conditions explain standing waves. Air\nresonators use related mathematics.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Sound Waves and Acoustic\nResonance<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/16-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 16 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§ 16.1, 16.5; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L5 – Interference and Standing Waves · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-03sc-physics-iii-vibrations-and-waves-fall-2016/pages/resource-index/\">8.03SC Physics III – Resource Index (2016)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Part I, Lectures 1–6 and 9; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L5 – Interference and Standing Waves · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M4-L6 – Sound Waves and Acoustic Resonance<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Waves, superposition and boundary\nconditions. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe sound as a pressure\nwave and calculate sound pressure level and air-column resonances.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Compression Waves in Fluids<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Sound<\/strong> in gases and liquids is a mechanical\nlongitudinal wave: small variations in density and pressure propagate\nthrough compression and rarefaction. Individual particles oscillate\nabout their mean positions while the pressure disturbance travels\nonward. Mechanical sound requires a medium and cannot propagate through\nan ideal vacuum. Its speed depends on the elastic response and density\nof the medium. In air, temperature, composition and humidity matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sound Speed and Logarithmic Level<\/h2>\n<p>For small pressure disturbances in a fluid, sound speed can be\nmodelled by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\sqrt{B/\\rho}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the appropriate bulk modulus and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ρ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe mass density. In an ideal gas under approximately adiabatic\nconditions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>M<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=\\sqrt{\\gamma RT/M}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith heat-capacity ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nuniversal gas constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntemperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand molar mass\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAcoustic intensity is power per area. Its level is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>I<\/mi><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_I=10\\log_{10}\\!\\left(\\frac{I}{I_0}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{dB}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Sound pressure level, at a common reference impedance, instead uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_p=20\\log_{10}(p_{\\mathrm{rms}}/p_0)\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSound pressure and acoustic intensity are not interchangeable.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Open–Open Pipe<\/h2>\n<p>Consider an ideal pipe open at both ends, length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.85<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.85\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith assumed sound speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\npressure nodes occur at both open ends. Allowed frequencies are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_n=nc/(2L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=200\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>400<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_2=400\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the first and second spatial <strong>pressure mode\nshapes<\/strong>; these must not be confused with air-particle\ndisplacement patterns. Real openings require end corrections, so the\neffective resonator length differs slightly.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_005_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two acoustic pressure modes with nodes at the open ends\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two acoustic pressure modes with nodes at\nthe open ends<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>This drawing shows pressure deviations, not air-particle\ndisplacements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find the fundamental frequency of an open–open pipe with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.50\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=340\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By how many dB does intensity level rise when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases by a factor of 100?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the difference between a pressure node and a displacement\nnode?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.50<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>340<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f_1=340/(2\\cdot0.50)=340\\,\\mathrm{Hz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mi>I<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta L_I=10\\log_{10}(100)=20\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A pressure node has zero pressure fluctuation; a displacement node\nhas zero particle displacement. In ideal pipe modes they do not occur at\nthe same position.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hearing, Intensity and Limits<\/h2>\n<p>A rise of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{dB}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin intensity level corresponds to ten times the intensity, not ten times\nthe perceived loudness. Human hearing weights frequencies differently;\nscales such as dB(A) introduce additional frequency-dependent weighting.\nA one-dimensional pipe model is approximate and requires a geometry and\nfrequency range compatible with plane waves. In rooms, reflections,\nabsorption and multiple modes shape the sound field. Levels should\ntherefore be reported with their reference quantity and measurement\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Acoustic resonators connect waves and material properties. Fluid\nmechanics additionally describes flow and pressure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Hydrostatics, Continuity and\nBernoulli’s Equation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/17-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 17 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§ 17.1–17.4; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L6 – Sound Waves and Acoustic Resonance · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M4-L7 – Hydrostatics, Continuity and Bernoulli’s Equation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Conservation of energy and elementary\nfield quantities. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply hydrostatic pressure, mass\nconservation and Bernoulli’s equation to ideal flows.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Pressure at Rest and in Motion<\/h2>\n<p>Fluids cannot sustain static shear stresses over long times as\nelastic solids do; they flow. Density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=m/V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand pressure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=F_\\perp/A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare basic state variables. In a resting liquid of constant density,\npressure rises with depth:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(h)=p_0+\\rho gh<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis hydrostatic contribution does not depend on container shape if the\nfluid is homogeneous and in equilibrium. Motion introduces velocity\nfields and transport of matter. Analysis requires an explicit system\nboundary and assumptions about compressibility and friction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mass Flow and Energy Balance<\/h2>\n<p>For steady incompressible flow without branches between two pipe\ncross-sections, mass conservation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_1v_1=A_2v_2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong a streamline of steady, inviscid flow at constant density,\nBernoulli’s equation reads<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>ρ<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p+\\tfrac12\\rho v^2+\\rho gz=\\mathrm{const}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis static pressure,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>ρ<\/mi><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tfrac12\\rho v^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nkinetic energy per volume and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho gz<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngravitational potential energy per volume. Under more restrictive\nirrotational conditions, the constant can be shared between streamlines.\nThe relation must not be applied unchanged across pumps, turbines,\nviscous losses or shocks.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Flow Through a Constriction<\/h2>\n<p>Water flows steadily and without friction through a horizontal pipe\nin an idealisation. Cross-sectional areas are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_1=8.0\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_2=4.0\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith initial speed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_1=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nContinuity requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_2=A_1v_1/A_2=4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat equal height yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msubsup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_1-p_2=\\tfrac12\\rho(v_2^2-v_1^2)=6000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the constriction, speed increases while static pressure drops in\nthe ideal case. The figure depicts the two sections, streamlines and the\nvelocity–pressure comparison.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_017_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Pipe constriction with streamlines, velocity and pressure comparison\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Pipe constriction with streamlines,\nvelocity and pressure comparison<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Continuity raises speed in the narrower section; static pressure\nfalls in the ideal case.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What additional hydrostatic pressure is produced by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof water at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A pipe’s cross-sectional area is halved; how does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange for steady incompressible flow?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why can the actual pressure loss exceed the ideal value in the\nexample?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>9.81<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>29<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mn>430<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=\\rho gh=1000\\cdot9.81\\cdot3=29\\,430\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Speed doubles if there is no branch flow.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Friction, vortex formation and local shape losses dissipate\nmechanical energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>When the Equation Fails<\/h2>\n<p>Bernoulli’s equation describes an <strong>idealised flow<\/strong>,\nnot a universal pressure rule for every real pipe system. Friction\ncreates additional pressure losses; strong acceleration may accompany\ncavitation, flow separation or turbulence. For gases, incompressibility\nmay be inappropriate. Pressure values must distinguish absolute pressure\nfrom gauge pressure. The equation is most useful when its domain of\nvalidity is checked explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Mass and energy balances describe ideal flows. The next lesson adds\nviscosity and the transition to turbulence.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Viscosity, Laminar Flow and\nReynolds Number<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-6-bernoullis-equation\">University Physics Volume 1 – 14.6 Bernoulli’s Equation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 14.6, introduction and learning objectives; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L7 – Hydrostatics, Continuity and Bernoulli’s Equation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 14 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§ 14.1, 14.5; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L7 – Hydrostatics, Continuity and Bernoulli’s Equation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M4-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M4-L8 – Viscosity, Laminar Flow and Reynolds Number<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M4 – Bachelor: Oscillations, Waves and Fluids<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Continuity, force balance and\ncircular pipe geometry. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M4&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive Poiseuille flow,\ncalculate hydraulic resistance and interpret Reynolds number.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Internal Friction in Fluids<\/h2>\n<p>A real fluid transfers shear forces between adjacent layers. In a\nNewtonian fluid the local shear stress is proportional to velocity\ngradient, with dynamic viscosity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the proportionality factor. For laminar, fully developed flow, no\nslip at a stationary pipe wall is generally a good approximation:\nvelocity vanishes at the wall and is largest in the centre. The\n<strong>Poiseuille model<\/strong> describes this smooth profile and\nmakes the strong dependence of volume flow on pipe radius explicit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Hagen–Poiseuille Law<\/h2>\n<p>For steady laminar, fully developed flow of an incompressible\nNewtonian fluid through a long straight pipe of radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\naxial momentum balance produces a parabolic velocity profile. The volume\nflow rate is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\frac{\\pi R^4\\Delta p}{8\\eta L},\\qquad\nv(r)=\\frac{\\Delta p}{4\\eta L}(R^2-r^2).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the positive pressure drop across\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis volume per time. Hydraulic resistance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>η<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R=\\Delta p/Q=8\\eta L/(\\pi R^4)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngrows strongly as radius decreases. The formula does not automatically\napply to turbulent flow, strongly varying viscosity or entrance regions.\nThe dimensionless quantity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Re}=\\rho v_{\\mathrm{mean}}D/\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompares inertial and viscous effects.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked Example: Pipe Resistance<\/h2>\n<p>For an idealised pipe with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1.0\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=1.0\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p=1000\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nPoiseuille’s formula gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.93<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\frac{\\pi(10^{-3})^4(1000)}{8(10^{-3})(1.0)}\\approx3.93\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm{m^3/s}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\pi R^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.125<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{mean}}=Q/A\\approx0.125\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=2R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>250<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Re}\\approx250<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a typical smooth pipe this lies in the laminar regime, making the\nmodel assumptions at least plausible. The figure illustrates the\nparabolic profile and the zero-velocity wall boundary.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_220_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Circular pipe cross-section showing a parabolic Poiseuille velocity profile\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Circular pipe cross-section showing a\nparabolic Poiseuille velocity profile<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Velocity is zero at the wall and maximal at the pipe\ncentre.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif pipe radius doubles at constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Re}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">3<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=1000\\,\\mathrm{kg/m^3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v_{\\mathrm{mean}}=0.20\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=0.010\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which assumption is violated by strongly turbulent pipe flow?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>R<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q\\propto R^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nmodel volume flow becomes sixteen times as large.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.010<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.001<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Re}=1000\\cdot0.20\\cdot0.010/0.001=2000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The assumption of steady, fully developed laminar Poiseuille flow\ndoes not hold.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reynolds Number Is Not a Universal Threshold<\/h2>\n<p>Reynolds number expresses a ratio of characteristic terms, not a\nuniversal boundary between two states. Transition depends on wall\nroughness, inlet disturbances, geometry and other conditions. In a\nstraight circular pipe, transitional behaviour often appears at Reynolds\nnumbers of a few thousand, but there is no universally valid single\nvalue. Turbulence changes the time-averaged profile and introduces\nfluctuating quantities. The Hagen–Poiseuille equation should therefore\nalways be used together with its assumptions, characteristic scales and\nflow regime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Viscosity and geometry determine hydraulic resistance. Transitions\nand losses must be considered for real systems.<\/p>\n<p>The next module extends these foundations to other areas of\nphysics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-7-viscosity-and-turbulence\">University Physics Volume 1 – 14.7 Viscosity and Turbulence<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · § 14.7, learning objectives; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L8 – Viscosity, Laminar Flow and Reynolds Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/14-summary\">University Physics Volume 1 – Chapter 14 Summary<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · §§ 14.1, 14.5; Subject relevance to lesson: M4-L8 – Viscosity, Laminar Flow and Reynolds Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
